<h2>Why Front Range sewer lines fail</h2>
<p>The pipe between your house and the public main has a hard life in Colorado. Much of the metro area sits on clay soils that swell when they get wet and shrink back as they dry, and some of that clay is bentonite, which moves a lot. Every season of heave and settlement works on the joints of a buried pipe. Over decades, joints pull apart, sections drop out of alignment, and low spots form where the soil gave way.</p>
<p>What the pipe is made of depends on when the house went up. Older Denver homes often have cast iron under the basement floor and out to the street, and that iron rusts from the inside until the bottom of the pipe thins or opens. The postwar subdivisions that spread across the metro from the late 1940s into the early 1970s were mostly built with short sections of clay tile, and some got Orangeburg, a fiber pipe soaked in tar that slowly loses its round shape under soil load.</p>
<p>Then there are the trees. Silver maple, cottonwood and Siberian elm were planted along streets and in back yards across the region, and their roots go straight for the moisture and nutrients leaking from a clay joint. A thread of root turns into a mat that catches paper and grease, and the first sign most owners get is a floor drain backing up in the basement.</p>

<h2>What trenchless means here</h2>
<p>A conventional replacement opens the ground along the whole length of the line. Because Colorado sewers are buried below frost depth, that trench is often deep, and it tends to run under whatever the owner cares about most: a finished basement, a mature tree, a front walk, a driveway, or the street itself.</p>
<p>Trenchless methods rebuild the pipe from inside, working through an existing cleanout or a small pit at each end. All Sewer and Plumbing Services has been doing this work in Colorado since 2009. Our owner, Jeff Haug, trained on Perma-Liner lining equipment and bought it at a time when very few contractors in the state offered lining at all. We are based in Westminster and run dedicated trenchless trucks and crews.</p>

<h2>The methods we use, and how we choose</h2>
<h3>Camera inspection comes first</h3>
<p>No method gets recommended until we have watched the whole line on video and located the problem areas from above ground. The footage tells us the material, the depth, where the joints have shifted and whether the pipe still falls steadily toward the main. <a href="/sewer-camera-inspection/">More on camera inspection.</a></p>

<h3>Hydro jetting and drain cleaning</h3>
<p>Sometimes a line only needs to be cleared of roots and grease. When a repair is coming, cleaning to bare pipe wall is also the required first step before lining. <a href="/hydro-jetting/">More on hydro jetting and drain cleaning.</a></p>

<h3>CIPP lining with Perma-Liner</h3>
<p>A resin-saturated liner is placed inside the old pipe and cured into a continuous, jointless tube. It suits pipe that is cracked, corroded or root-infested but still holds its shape and its slope. <a href="/cipp-pipe-lining/">More on CIPP lining.</a></p>

<h3>Pipe bursting with fused HDPE</h3>
<p>A bursting head breaks the old pipe apart while pulling a new, heat-fused polyethylene line into the same path. It is how we replace pipe that is too broken to line, without a trench between the two pits. <a href="/pipe-bursting/">More on pipe bursting.</a></p>

<h3>Spot repairs and conventional replacement</h3>
<p>When the damage is confined to one section, we can dig at that spot, replace the bad piece, and often line the rest. When grade is lost along the run, excavation is the honest answer. <a href="/sewer-line-replacement/">More on sewer line replacement.</a></p>

<h2>When trenchless is the wrong answer</h2>
<p>A liner copies the shape of the pipe it cures inside. That is a strength on a cracked but straight line and a problem anywhere the pipe has sagged. In expansive clay it is common to find a belly where water sits, a stretch that has shifted and lost its slope, a section that has frozen and split, or a joint so far out of line that nothing can pass smoothly. Lining those spots locks the defect in place. Bursting can correct some of them; others need a dig. We would rather tell you that up front than sell a repair that backs up again.</p>

<h2>Who owns the line, and who sets the rules</h2>
<p>In most Front Range cities and districts, the property owner is responsible for the entire service line from the house to the public main, including the tap and the portion under the street. That surprises a lot of people, and it matters when a camera shows damage past the curb.</p>
<p>The rules are also local. Parts of the metro are served by city utilities, and many suburban and unincorporated areas are served by special sanitation districts that set their own permitting, inspection and material requirements. Some districts restrict certain lining methods. Before we book a job, All Sewer confirms what the city or district requires for that address so the repair is approved the first time.</p>

<h2>How a job runs</h2>
<p>The sequence is the same on a small house or a commercial building: inspect and locate, clean, choose the method and write up the scope, handle permits and district requirements, do the work, then run the camera again so you can see the result. The full walk-through is on <a href="/our-process/">our process page</a>, and common questions are answered in the <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a>.</p>

